Submission: By Euclid's theorem on the infinitude of primes, there are infinitely many primes. \textcolor{green}{This is a good starting point, as it establishes the availability of primes for constructing sums.} By looking at divisibility by two and three, we can see that all primes greater than 3 are either 1 or 5 modulo 6. \textcolor{green}{Correct observation about the form of primes greater than 3.} Therefore, there are \emph{either} infinitely many primes of the form $6k+1$ or infinitely many primes of the form $6k+5$. \textcolor{red}{This statement is incorrect. There are infinitely many primes of both forms, not just one or the other.} Let $S$ denote an infinite set of such primes in either case. If the first case holds, then $p + 5$ is divisible by 6 for any $p \in S$. \textcolor{red}{This is incorrect. If $p \equiv 1 \pmod{6}$, then $p + 5 \equiv 0 \pmod{6}$, but 5 is not a prime number.} If the second case holds, then $p + 7$ is divisible by 6 for any $p \in S$. \textcolor{red}{Similarly, this is incorrect. If $p \equiv 5 \pmod{6}$, then $p + 7 \equiv 0 \pmod{6}$, but 7 is not a prime number.} In either case, we have found infinitely many numbers that are divisible by 6 and that can be written as the sum of two prime numbers. \textcolor{red}{The conclusion is based on incorrect reasoning. Consider instead that for any prime $p \equiv 1 \pmod{6}$, $p + (p+5) \equiv 0 \pmod{6}$, and both $p$ and $p+5$ can be prime for infinitely many $p$.}

\textcolor{green}{To correct the approach, consider pairs of primes such as $(3, 3)$, $(5, 7)$, $(11, 13)$, etc., where the sum is divisible by 6. There are infinitely many such pairs.}

